      trivia: "The original four models all bear the names of fortresses that guarded the port of Havana in colonial times, and the additions are unified under the street names of the old town — a marque that shifts its theme from fortress to street while telling, without interruption, of “the city of Havana” through band and vitola name. Atop the tower of the Real Fuerza fortress, source of one vitola's name, stands “La Giraldilla”, the female-figure weather vane known as a symbol of Havana, whose image is also used on the label of Havana Club rum. As with the “1519” humidors of the 500th anniversary, serially numbered from 1519 to 2019, the habit of engraving the city's growth rings into the smallest details of its commemoratives runs right through, and it is described as a marca that condenses the memory of a city into a single box."
